2: \nL533: 185\nL534: \nL535: 186\nL536: \nL537: 187\nL538: \nL539: 188\nL540: \nL541: 189\nL542: \nL543: 190\nL544: \nL545: 191\nL546: 192\nL547: \nL548: 193\nL549: \nL550: 194\nL551: \nL552: 195\nL553: \nL554: 196\nL555: \nL556: 197\nL557: \nL558: 198\nL559: \nL560: 199\nL561: \nL562: 200\nL563: \nL564: 201\nL565: \nL566: 202\nL567: \nL568: 203\nL569: \nL570: 204\nL571: \nL572: 205\nL573: \nL574: 206\nL575: \nL576: 207\nL577: \nL578: 208\nL579: \nL580: 209\nL581: \nL582: 210\nL583: \nL584: 211\nL585: \nL586: 212\nL587: \nL588: 213\nL589: \nL590: 214\nL591: \nL592: 215\nL593: \nL594: 216\nL595: \nL596: 217\nL597: \nL598: 218\nL599: \nL600: 219\nL601: \nL602: 220\nL603: \nL604: 221\nL605: \nL606: 222\nL607: \nL608: 223\nL609: \nL610: 224\nL611: \nL612: 225\nL613: \nL614: 226\nL615: \nL616: 227\nL617: \nL618: 228\nL619: \nL620: 229\nL621: \nL622: 230\nL623: \nL624: 231\nL625: \nL626: 232\nL627: \nL628: 233\nL629: \nL630: 234\nL631: \nL632: 235\nL633: \nL634: 236\nL635: \nL636: 237\nL637: \nL638: 238\nL639: \nL640: 239\nL641: \nL642: 240\nL643: \nL644: 241\nL645: \nL646: 242\nL647: \nL648: 243\nL649: \nL650: 244\nL651: \nL652: 245\nL653: \nL654: 246\nL655: \nL656: 247\nL657: \nL658: 248\nL659: \nL660: 249\nL661: \nL662: 250\nL663: \nL664: 251\nL665: \nL666: 252\nL667: \nL668: 253\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://sites.math.rutgers.edu/~weibel/Kbook/Kbook.ii.pdf)\nciteturn1358view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 100; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://sites.math.rutgers.edu/~weibel/Kbook/Kbook.ii.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 6390\nL0@P0: CHAPTER II\nL1@P0: THE GROTHENDIECK GROUP K0\nL2@P0: There are several ways to construct the “Grothendieck group” of a mathematical\nL3@P0: object. We begin with the group completion version, because it has been the most\nL4@P0: historically important. After giving the applications to rings and topological spaces,\nL5@P0: we discuss λ-operations in §4. In sections 6 and 7 we describe the Grothendieck\nL6@P0: group of an “exact category,” and apply it to the K-theory of schemes in §8. This\nL7@P0: construction is generalized to the Grothendieck group of a “Waldhausen category”\nL8@P0: in §9.\nL9@P0: §1. The Group Completion of a monoid\nL10@P0: Both K0(R) and K0(X) are formed by taking the group completion of an abelian\nL11@P0: monoid—the monoid P(R) of f.g. projective R-modules and the monoid VB(X) of\nL12@P0: vector bundles over X, respectively. We begin with a description of this construc\u0002tion.\nL13@P0: Recall that an abelian monoid is a set M together with an associative, commu\u0002tative operation + and an “additive” identity element 0. A monoid map f: M → N\nL14@P0: is a set map such that f(0) = 0 and f(m + m0) = f(m) + f(m0). The most famous\nL15@P0: example of an abelian monoid is N = {0, 1, 2, ...}, the natural numbers with additive\nL16@P0: identity zero. If A is an abelian group then not only is A an abelian monoid, but\nL17@P0: so is any additively closed subset of A containing 0.\nL18@P0: The group completion of an abelian monoid M is an abelian group M−1M,\nL19@P0: together with a monoid map [ ]: M → M−1M which is universal in the sense that,\nL20@P0: for every abelian group A and every monoid map α: M → A, there is a unique\nL21@P0: abelian group homomorphism ˜α: M−1M → A such that ˜α([m]) = α(m) for all\nL22@P0: m ∈ M.\nL23@P0: For example, the group completion of N is Z. If A is an abelian group then\nL24@P0: clearly A−1A = A; if M is a submonoid of A (additively closed subset containing\nL25@P0: 0), then M−1M is the subgroup of A generated by M.\nL26@P0: Every abelian monoid M has a group completion. One way to contruct it is to\nL27@P0: form the free abelian group F(M) on symbols [m], m ∈ M, and then factor out by\nL28@P0: the subgroup R(M) generated by the relations [m + n] − [m] − [n]. By universality,\nL29@P0: if M → N is a monoid map, the map M → N → N −1N extends uniquely to a\nL30@P0: homomorphism from M−1M to N −1N. Thus group completion is a functor from\nL31@P0: abelian monoids to abelian groups. A little decoding shows that in fact it is left\nL32@P0: adjoint to the forgetful functor, because of the natural isomorphism\nL33@P0: Hommonoids(M, A) ∼= Homgroups(M−1M, A).\nL34@P0: Typeset by AMS-TEX\nL35@P0-1: 12 II. THE GROTHENDIECK GROUP K0\nL36@P1: Proposition 1.1. Let M be an abelian monoid. Then:\nL37@P1: (a) Every element of M−1M is of the form [m] − [n] for some m, n ∈ M;\nL38@P1: (b) If m, n ∈ M then [m] = [n] in M−1M iff m + p = n + p for some p ∈ M;\nL39@P1: (c) The monoid map M ×M → M−1M sending (m, n) to [m]−[n] is surjective.\nL40@P1: (d) Hence M−1M is the set-theoretic quotient of M × M by the equivalence\nL41@P1: relation generated by (m, n) ∼ (m + p, n + p).\nL42@P1: Proof. Every element of a free abelian group is a difference of sums of genera\u0002tors, and in F(M) we have ([m1] + [m2] + · · ·) ≡ [m1 + m2 + · · · ] modulo R(M).\nL43@P1: Hence every element of M−1M is a difference of generators. This establishes (a)\nL44@P1: and (c). For (b), suppose that [m] − [n] = 0 in M−1M. Then in the free abelian\nL45@P1: group F(M) we have\nL46@P1: [m] − [n] = X([ai + bi] − [ai] − [bi]) −\nL47@P1: X([cj + dj ] − [cj ] − [dj ]).\nL48@P1: Translating negative terms to the other side yields the following equation:\nL49@P1: (∗) [m] +X([ai] + [bi]) +X[cj + dj ] = [n] +X[ai + bi] +X([cj ] + [dj ]).\nL50@P1: Now in a free abelian group two sums of generators P[xi] and P[yj ] can only\nL51@P1: be equal if they have the same number of terms, and the generators differ by a\nL52@P1: permutation σ in the sense that yi = xσ(i). Hence the generators on the left and\nL53@P1: right of (∗) differ only by a permutation. This means that in M the sum of the\nL54@P1: terms on the left and right of (∗) are the same, i.e.,\nL55@P1: m +\nL56@P1: X(ai + bi) +X(cj + dj ) = n +X(ai + bi) +X(cj + dj )\nL57@P1: in M. This yields (b), and part (d) follows from (a) and (b).\nL58@P1: The two corollaries below are immediate from Proposition 1.1, given the following\nL59@P1: definitions. A cancellation monoid is an abelian monoid M such that for all m, n, p ∈\nL60@P1: M, m + p = n + p implies m = n. A submonoid L of an abelian monoid M is\nL61@P1: called cofinal if for every m ∈ M there is an m0 ∈ M so that m + m0 ∈ L.\nL62@P1: Corollary 1.2. M injects into M−1M if and only if M is a cancellation\nL63@P1: monoid.\nL64@P1: Corollary 1.3. If L is cofinal in an abelian monoid M, then:\nL65@P1: (a) L\nL66@P1: −1L is a subgroup of M−1M;\nL67@P1: (b) Every element of M−1M is of the form [m] − [`] for some m ∈ M, ` ∈ L;\nL68@P1: (c) If [m] = [m0] in M−1M then m + ` = m0 + ` for some ` ∈ L.\n"
